WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.950
In this video we’re going to look at the enzymes involved in digestion in more detail.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:13.950
The large molecules which make up our food, like lipids, proteins, and carbohydrates, are too big to be moved into our blood,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.950
so they need to be digested into smaller molecules physically, by processes like chewing,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.950
and chemically, by special proteins called enzymes.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.950
Different types of enzymes digest the different types of food, so let’s look at the food groups in turn.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.950
First, carbohydrates.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.950
Foods like rice and pasta are made of carbohydrates.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:41.950
The simplest carbohydrates are sugars, which can be joined into big chains to make complex carbohydrates like starch.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.950
Carbohydrates are digested by carbohydrase enzymes such as amylase.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:54.950
Amylase is a special type of carbohydrase which breaks down starch, a big carbohydrate, into smaller molecules.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.950
These can then be broken down further into glucose, which is small enough to be moved into the blood.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.950
Amylase is found in your saliva, which is where carbohydrates will first start to be broken down chemically,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:14.950
and another amylase is released into the small intestine from the pancreas, so much further down your digestive system.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.950
Now let’s look at proteins. Proteins are found in meat, fish, beans, and pulses.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.950
They are made up of amino acids and are digested by protease enzymes.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.950
The protease enzymes break down proteins into amino acids.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:37.950
A protease called pepsin breaks down proteins in the stomach, but this doesn’t work once the food moves into the small intestine.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.950
In the small intestine the conditions are different, so a different protease called trypsin

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.950
is released into the small intestine from the pancreas for continued protein digestion.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.950
What conditions do you think might be different in the small intestine compared to the stomach?

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.950
Whilst the stomach is very acidic and has a pH of about two, the small intestine has a higher pH of about eight.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.950
Protease enzymes which work well at pH 2 don’t work at pH 8,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.950
which is why different protease enzymes are needed in the small intestine.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:14.009
And the last group of food: lipids.
